# Chapter 5 · Women as Representatives: Inside the Assemblies

> The syllabus names "**gender issues in women as representatives**". The ==2022== question asked: "In your opinion, what are the main issues faced by women as representatives in Pakistan?" The 2021 question asked whether the quota is "showing results".

## 5.1  How many women sit in Pakistan's legislatures

::: table | Women's seats in Pakistan's legislatures.
@widths 4,2.4,2.4,5.2
House | Total seats | Reserved for women | Notes
National Assembly | 336 | 60 | 266 general seats (11 women won in 2024) and 10 for non-Muslims
Senate | 96 | 17 | 4 per province and 1 for Islamabad; 18 women senators in 2024
Punjab Assembly | 371 | 66 | Largest provincial reserved quota
Sindh Assembly | 168 | 29 | —
Khyber Pakhtunkhwa Assembly | 145 | 26 | Increased after the merger of the tribal areas (2018)
Balochistan Assembly | 65 | 11 | —
:::

With the 60 reserved seats and a handful of general-seat winners, women hold roughly **one-fifth** of the National Assembly — about the **world average** of a little over a quarter, but far below parity.

::: simple
Reserved seats are **not** contested directly by voters. After the election, each party gets a share of the women's seats **in proportion to the general seats it won**, and fills them from a **priority list** of women it submitted earlier. So a reserved-seat member owes her seat to her **party**, not to a constituency.
:::

## 5.2  Two classes of women members

Pakistan's system creates two kinds of women legislators:

::: fig compare | Women on general seats and women on reserved seats.
## General-seat women
- Elected directly by voters in a constituency
- Have a constituency, voters and local networks
- Receive development funds and local influence
- Often from political families; few in number (11 in 2024)
## Reserved-seat women
- Chosen by the party from a priority list
- No constituency; owe the seat to the party leader
- Often seen as "second-class" members
- Many are first-time, educated, urban professionals
:::

## 5.3  The main issues faced by women representatives (2022)

::: fig grid | The main issues faced by women representatives in Pakistan.
@cols 3
Legitimacy: reserved seats seen as "nominated", not elected
No constituency: no voters to answer to or build on
Party control: loyalty to leaders over women's issues
Selection: lists favour relatives of party leaders
Unequal resources: smaller development funds and offices
Marginal roles: few chairs of key committees or ministries
Harassment: sexist remarks, character attacks, online abuse
Double burden: family duties and late sessions
Re-election: few move on to general seats
:::

### (1) Legitimacy and the "nominated" label

Because reserved-seat women are not directly elected, colleagues and the public often treat them as **nominated** rather than elected — "*token*" members. Male legislators have sometimes dismissed their views on the floor of the House.

### (2) No constituency

Without a constituency, reserved-seat women have no voters to serve, no local base to build, and no clear way to show performance. This also makes it hard for them to win a **general** seat later.

### (3) Dependence on party leaders

Reserved seats are filled from **party lists** drawn up by party leaderships. Places on the list often go to **relatives** of party leaders, loyal workers or donors' families. Since their future depends on the leader's favour, women members find it risky to break party line — even on women's issues. A dispute in ==2024–25== over whether reserved seats should go to PTI-backed independents, which reached the Supreme Court, showed how completely the seats depend on **party arithmetic**.

### (4) Unequal resources and roles

Women members, especially on reserved seats, have often received smaller shares of **development funds**, fewer **committee chairs**, and fewer **ministries** — usually "soft" portfolios such as women's development, social welfare or education rather than finance, interior or foreign affairs. In ==2025== there were **no women federal ministers**.

### (5) Harassment and sexism

Women legislators have faced **sexist remarks** in the assemblies, comments on their appearance, and character attacks in the media and online. Such incidents, often widely reported, discourage women from politics.

### (6) Family duties and working conditions

Long, late sessions, travel and the absence of **childcare** facilities in assemblies make the work harder for women with families.

### (7) Capacity and information

Many reserved-seat women are first-time legislators. Without training, research staff and access to information, they find it hard to draft bills, scrutinise budgets and use parliamentary procedures — though training programmes by parliament, NGOs and donors have helped.

## 5.4  How women representatives have responded

::: case The Women's Parliamentary Caucus (2008) | working across party lines
Formed in ==2008== under the patronage of **Dr Fahmida Mirza**, the first woman Speaker of the National Assembly (MCQs 2016, 2019), the caucus brought women members of all parties together. It pushed the laws of ==2010–2016==: the **workplace harassment law** (==2010==), the **Acid Control** and **Anti-Women Practices** laws (==2011==), the **NCSW Act** (==2012==) and the **honour-killing** and **anti-rape** laws (==2016==). Provincial assemblies formed their own caucuses.
-> The best example of **substantive representation** and of **critical actors** at work.
:::

Women legislators have also been among the most **active** members in parliamentary monitoring by **FAFEN** and **PILDAT** — moving questions, resolutions and private members' bills out of proportion to their numbers.

## 5.4a  Women in the Senate and the provincial assemblies

- **Senate:** women's **17** seats (four per province and one for Islamabad) are filled by the provincial and national assemblies by **single transferable vote**, so Senate women also depend on party strength. Women senators have introduced important bills — the anti-honour-killing bill of ==2014== was moved by Senator **Sughra Imam**.
- **Provincial assemblies:** the **132** reserved seats give women a presence in all four provinces. Provincial women's caucuses pushed the **domestic violence laws** of **Sindh** (==2013==), **Balochistan** (==2014==), **Punjab** (==2016==) and **Khyber Pakhtunkhwa** (==2021==). Since the **18th Amendment** moved most social policy to the provinces, provincial women legislators matter more than ever.
- **Local councils:** women councillors deal with water, sanitation, schools and health — the issues closest to women's daily lives.

## 5.5  Notable women in Pakistan's legislatures and offices

::: table | Pakistani women "firsts" in governance (many are MCQ answers).
@widths 2,5,7
Year | Person | First
1947 | Jahanara Shahnawaz, Shaista Ikramullah | The two women members of the first Constituent Assembly
1965 | Fatima Jinnah | First woman to contest a presidential election (against Ayub Khan)
1973 | Ra'ana Liaquat Ali Khan | First woman governor (Sindh)
1988 | Benazir Bhutto | First woman prime minister
2008 | Fahmida Mirza | First woman Speaker of the National Assembly
2011 | Hina Rabbani Khar | First woman foreign minister
2017 | Tehmina Janjua | First woman foreign secretary (MCQ 2018)
2022 | Ayesha Malik | First woman judge of the Supreme Court
2024 | Maryam Nawaz | First woman chief minister (Punjab)
:::

## 5.5a  Lessons from other parliaments

- **Rwanda:** after the ==1994== genocide, the ==2003== constitution reserved **30%** of seats for women; women now hold **over 60%** of the lower house — the highest share in the world — and have led reforms on inheritance and gender-based violence.
- **The Nordic countries:** voluntary **party quotas** and proportional representation produced women's shares of 40% or more, alongside childcare and parental-leave policies that make political careers compatible with family life.
- **The United Kingdom:** the Labour Party's **all-women shortlists** in selected constituencies helped elect **101** Labour women MPs in ==1997==, more than doubling women's representation at a stroke.
- **Nepal:** a constitutional one-third with PR lists produced about **35%** women in ==2026==, but only 14 won directly — showing that list quotas can raise numbers without breaking the barrier on direct seats.

The lesson for Pakistan is that **design** matters: quotas linked to direct election or winnable constituencies, party rules and family-friendly parliaments all change what women can do once elected.

## 5.6  Making representation work

1. **Constituencies for reserved seats** — options include giving reserved-seat members defined areas, or moving to **direct election** of women's seats (e.g. reserved constituencies rotating every election, as India's panchayats do).
2. **Transparent party lists** — open criteria and party elections for list places.
3. **Equal resources** — equal development funds, offices and staff for all members.
4. **Committee roles and ministries** — women chairs of key committees; women in "hard" portfolios.
5. **Codes of conduct** — sanctions for sexist remarks and harassment in the assemblies.
6. **Family-friendly assemblies** — day-care centres, sensible sitting hours.
7. **Capacity** — research support, training and mentoring.

::: balance | Are women representatives effective?
- **Yes:** the caucus passed landmark laws; women members are active in debates and questions; their presence has made violence and harassment normal business of parliament.
- **Limited:** women are dependent on party leaders; few hold real power; reserved seats have not produced many general-seat winners; issues such as economic policy remain male domains.
-> **Line to take:** women representatives have made a **real difference on women's issues** despite **structural constraints**; the reforms above would let them do more.
:::
